insert into public.content_translations (content_id, language, title, body, application_text, challenge_text, prayer_text) values
('03be3dca-92d0-4098-8e05-aaba9e73c834', 'en', $tr$A blessing for today$tr$, $tr$This is an ancient blessing: may the Lord bless and keep you, make his face shine on you and be gracious to you, look on you with favor and give you peace. It can be read as a good wish for you and also as something to offer to someone close by. It ends with the word peace.$tr$, $tr$Who would benefit from hearing a kind word from you today?$tr$, $tr$Send a message of care or a blessing to someone.$tr$, $tr$Lord, bless and keep the people I love, and me too. May your peace be upon this day.$tr$),
('0d38c36c-c799-44c4-adeb-d90d939d157a', 'en', $tr$Comfort before the celebration$tr$, $tr$The text begins with a gentle command: comfort my people, speak tenderly to the heart of those who have suffered. Before any celebration comes comfort. Advent can be a time to admit that not everyone reaches the end of the year feeling light, and that comforting and being comforted are also part of the waiting.$tr$, $tr$Do you need comfort this year? Or do you know someone who does?$tr$, $tr$Call or visit someone who has gone through a loss or a hard time this year. If you're the one who needs comfort, talk with someone you trust.$tr$, $tr$Lord, bring comfort to those who are tired of crying, including me. Help me notice who needs a word of tenderness.$tr$),
('15251e9c-af17-46df-9bd5-c1444021d886', 'en', $tr$Today is enough on its own$tr$, $tr$Matthew 6:34 says not to worry about tomorrow, because "each day has enough trouble of its own". It isn't about ignoring the future — it's about not carrying the weight of every day all at once. You don't need to solve the whole week right now. This moment, today, is already enough.$tr$, $tr$Notice how many times today you've already started thinking about something that will only happen tomorrow, next week, or a month from now. You don't need to solve it now — just noticing that you're doing it already helps.$tr$, $tr$Whenever your mind goes to a "later" problem today, come back to one simple question: "What needs my attention right now?"$tr$, $tr$Help me keep my feet in today, without carrying the weight of what hasn't arrived yet. What belongs to today is already enough.$tr$),
('15d31e13-fe24-41fa-a562-af10b69d7e1c', 'en', $tr$Starting again is not failing$tr$, $tr$Lamentations 3:22-23 speaks of mercies that are renewed every morning. It doesn't talk about accumulated merit or a perfect streak — it talks about something that comes back with every new day, no matter what the day before was like. If you're picking this moment back up after time away, today counts just as much as any other day. You don't need to make up for what was left behind. Just start again.$tr$, $tr$Think of something you've been avoiding only because "too much time has already passed." The obstacle isn't the task itself — it's the idea that it's too late. Today is a good day to test whether that's really true.$tr$, $tr$Choose one small thing you've been putting off out of embarrassment about the time that passed, and do it today — without explaining yourself, without trying to catch up. Just do it.$tr$, $tr$Thank you for today being a new day, without my having to carry the weight of the days that came before. Help me start again without spending my energy trying to make up for what has already passed.$tr$),
('160d9384-b42b-4f1f-91e1-a9783941f2e6', 'en', $tr$Abiding$tr$, $tr$Jesus uses the image of the vine and the branches: whoever remains in him bears much fruit, and apart from him the branches can do nothing. The key word is remain: to return, to stay, to continue. What keeps a branch alive isn't one day's effort, but staying connected every day.$tr$, $tr$What helps you come back to this moment every day, even on busy days?$tr$, $tr$Choose a time for tomorrow's moment and put it on your calendar.$tr$, $tr$Lord, I want to remain close to you on good days and on busy days. Help me always come back, even when the day doesn't make it easy.$tr$),
('1e2a51a2-68d4-41de-9fe3-4e11a98f1ba0', 'en', $tr$Joseph's quiet yes$tr$, $tr$Joseph discovers that Mary is pregnant and considers quietly leaving her. An angel guides him in a dream, and he stays. The text barely records Joseph's words, only his actions: he welcomes, protects, and gives the name. One possible reading is that loving also means staying, even without understanding everything, and caring in silence.$tr$, $tr$What quiet kind of care can you offer someone today, without expecting recognition?$tr$, $tr$Do something useful for someone without announcing it and without expecting thanks.$tr$, $tr$Lord, teach me to care without fanfare. May I learn from those who love by staying and protecting.$tr$),
('20a826af-bb07-40de-b0fe-4046d6fb9ea7', 'en', $tr$Good news for those on night duty$tr$, $tr$The first announcement of the birth goes to shepherds working at night in the fields. It doesn't go to important people, but to those who were on duty. One possible reading is that the good news reaches those who work while others rest. If you are on duty this Christmas, the text also speaks to you.$tr$, $tr$Who is working today while you rest? Can you thank that person?$tr$, $tr$Thank someone who works on holidays: a doorman, a driver, a nurse, a clerk.$tr$, $tr$Father, bless those who work while others celebrate. May each of them feel that they were remembered too.$tr$),
('22e682d4-9c25-4869-9b7e-6fb718953d86', 'en', $tr$Strength in what looks like weakness$tr$, $tr$Paul shares that he heard that God's grace was enough for him, and that power is made perfect in weakness. Admitting limits here isn't failure: it's the place where help comes in. One possible reading is that you don't need to seem unshakable in order to be held up.$tr$, $tr$Which limit of yours have you been hiding because you think you should be able to handle it?$tr$, $tr$Admit a limit of yours today, to someone or to yourself, without making excuses.$tr$, $tr$Father, I have limits and often hide them. Thank you for holding me up anyway. Help me accept help.$tr$),
('25744c54-ea01-42a3-ae10-337c0de8d928', 'en', $tr$Work as an offering$tr$, $tr$Paul says to do your work wholeheartedly, as for the Lord and not for people. This gives value to ordinary tasks: washing, writing, serving, organizing. Doing things with attention is a way of giving meaning to what feels like routine.$tr$, $tr$Which routine task do you usually do on autopilot?$tr$, $tr$Do one routine task today with full attention, without doing other things at the same time.$tr$, $tr$Lord, may the simple tasks of my day also be a way of honoring you. Help me do them with care.$tr$),
('26c54ef9-c368-495b-896e-4a2bb55a2431', 'en', $tr$Forgiveness at the worst moment$tr$, $tr$From the cross, Jesus says: Father, forgive them, for they do not know what they are doing. It's a hard sentence to hear, spoken in the moment of pain. The text doesn't ask us to pretend the wrong wasn't wrong; it shows someone who chose not to return hatred. Forgiving is a path, sometimes a long one, and it doesn't have to happen today.$tr$, $tr$Is there someone you would like one day to be able to forgive? Wishing for that is already a beginning.$tr$, $tr$If there's a small hurt, think of one simple gesture of reconciliation. If it's a deep wound, talk with someone you trust.$tr$, $tr$Father, help me move toward forgiveness in my own time. Tend to my wounds and free me from resentment.$tr$),
('26f69264-3eda-4c9f-9dcc-8ff029795a92', 'en', $tr$Not everything needs to be understood$tr$, $tr$The proverb invites us to trust in the Lord with all our heart and not to lean on our own understanding. It isn't an invitation to switch off your mind: it's a reminder that there are decisions where we can't see the whole path. You can take the next step even without seeing the end.$tr$, $tr$Which decision is stuck waiting for complete certainty? Maybe one small step is enough for today.$tr$, $tr$Choose one pending decision and take the smallest possible step on it today.$tr$, $tr$Father, I don't always understand the path. Help me trust and take the next step calmly, without waiting to have all the answers.$tr$),
('2aef4e94-ac41-438d-baaa-db4c29cc0432', 'en', $tr$Not getting tired of doing good$tr$, $tr$Paul encourages us not to lose heart in doing good, because we will reap a harvest at the proper time, if we don't give up. The text admits that we feel like stopping and speaks of the proper time: not everything gives immediate results. Small, repeated gestures build something that everyday life doesn't let us see.$tr$, $tr$What small good have you been doing without seeing any results yet?$tr$, $tr$Do one discreet act of kindness today: a message, a help, a thank-you.$tr$, $tr$Lord, sometimes I get tired of doing good without seeing anything back. Help me keep going calmly and trust the proper time.$tr$),
('2d798f8a-689c-4e76-bae3-e60c12cc5c72', 'en', $tr$Made with care$tr$, $tr$The psalmist speaks of having been formed in the womb and praises God for being made in an extraordinary way. On days of comparison, the text reminds us of a worth that comes before any achievement, appearance, or productivity. One possible reading is that you don't need to earn your place: it was given to you first.$tr$, $tr$In what situations do you compare yourself to others and end up feeling less?$tr$, $tr$Write one thing about yourself you're thankful for, without qualifying it.$tr$, $tr$Lord, thank you for knowing me completely. Help me see myself with more kindness and stop measuring myself against others.$tr$),
('2dc5dc59-8930-4adf-9ad2-ec9ab4ad1419', 'en', $tr$Serving on your knees$tr$, $tr$Before the Passover feast, Jesus gets up from the table, takes off his outer garment, picks up a towel, and washes his disciples' feet. It was a task for the lowest of servants, and he chooses to do it. One possible reading is that, in this story, power shows up in serving. The text invites us to notice the small, invisible tasks that hold everyone's life together.$tr$, $tr$What humble task could you do for someone without expecting recognition?$tr$, $tr$Do a simple service today for someone at home or at work: wash the dishes, or help with a task.$tr$, $tr$Lord, teach me to serve without needing applause. May I find dignity in small tasks.$tr$),
('2deda955-23a4-40e8-ab45-d617904c08bb', 'en', $tr$Forgiving is a process too$tr$, $tr$Paul asks us to be kind and compassionate to one another, and to forgive, just as God forgave us in Christ. Forgiveness doesn't always happen all at once, and it doesn't require pretending it didn't hurt. It can start with something small: not feeding resentment today. If the situation involves something serious, talking with someone you trust is also a form of care.$tr$, $tr$Is there someone or something you've been carrying for a long time that you'd like to let go of, little by little?$tr$, $tr$Choose one small attitude today that doesn't feed an old resentment.$tr$, $tr$Lord, there is still something that hurts. Help me forgive in my own time, without pretending it didn't happen, and find peace.$tr$),
('3064dc73-0de6-47e7-9d5c-45878e459752', 'en', $tr$An answer that disarms$tr$, $tr$The proverb says a gentle answer turns away anger, while a harsh word stirs up more. In tense conversations, tone often decides more than the argument does. Answering gently isn't giving in: it's choosing not to feed the fire.$tr$, $tr$Which recent conversation or message would have gone differently with a calmer tone?$tr$, $tr$In a tense situation today, take a breath before you answer and choose a softer tone.$tr$, $tr$Father, help me answer calmly when I'm irritated. May my words help put out the fire, not light it.$tr$),
('37ddbf7f-6d98-4935-948d-a7214353e03d', 'en', $tr$Strength that returns$tr$, $tr$The text speaks of those who wait and have their strength renewed. The verse before it acknowledges that even the strongest grow tired, so tiredness isn't treated as failure. Waiting, here, doesn't mean standing still doing nothing: it means keeping on walking without the rush to solve everything alone.$tr$, $tr$Where have you been pushing yourself as if you couldn't ever get tired?$tr$, $tr$Today, take a real short break at some moment when you would normally keep going straight ahead.$tr$, $tr$Lord, I'm tired of some things and I won't pretend otherwise. Renew my strength little by little, in my own pace and in your time.$tr$),
('394311b2-0a57-4296-aa93-f14b7f3b4579', 'en', $tr$Resting is also following the shepherd$tr$, $tr$The psalm begins with the image of a shepherd who leads the sheep to green pastures and quiet waters. The first thing it shows is rest, not work. One possible reading is that being cared for comes before producing. If your day was hectic, this moment can simply be that: stopping for a while, without needing to prove anything to anyone.$tr$, $tr$Think of a point in your day where you rush out of habit, not out of necessity.$tr$, $tr$Before your next task, stop for 2 minutes, without your phone, just breathing.$tr$, $tr$Lord, thank you for caring for me even when I forget to stop. Help me rest without guilt and carry on the day with a lighter heart.$tr$),
('3cbcb118-02c8-42b0-be8c-1fd99d1c2778', 'en', $tr$They went and told$tr$, $tr$The shepherds go to see the child and then tell what they had heard. The text says that everyone who heard it was amazed. They didn't keep the news to themselves. Sharing what has done us good is a simple way of testifying, and it doesn't have to be a speech. It can be saying to someone: something good happened to me.$tr$, $tr$What good thing happened to you this year that is worth telling someone?$tr$, $tr$Tell one person about a good thing you lived through this year.$tr$, $tr$Father, give me simplicity to share the good I've received, without exaggeration and without fear.$tr$),
('4002e632-f3f3-4ed0-a33c-4a5cbb8bacce', 'en', $tr$Close to the broken$tr$, $tr$The psalm says the Lord is close to those whose hearts are broken and saves those who are crushed in spirit. If today feels heavy, the text speaks of closeness, not of a hurry to feel better. If the weight is great or has lasted a long time, talking with someone you trust, such as a friend, a leader, or a professional, is also a form of care.$tr$, $tr$Who could really know how you are doing today?$tr$, $tr$Tell someone you trust how you are really doing, in one sentence.$tr$, $tr$Lord, my heart is heavy today. Stay close to me and help me not to carry this alone.$tr$),
('4051a5bb-1042-4048-8fa1-54665ad9dbd5', 'en', $tr$Ask, seek, knock$tr$, $tr$Jesus invites us to ask, to seek, and to knock, and speaks of receiving, finding, and having the door opened. These are verbs of continuity: the text speaks of someone who doesn't stop looking. Asking is part of the relationship, and it doesn't need beautiful words.$tr$, $tr$What have you been holding on to that you haven't yet brought to prayer?$tr$, $tr$Ask for something you've been holding on to today, in simple words.$tr$, $tr$Father, I have things to ask you and I don't even know how to say them. Listen to me the way I'm able to speak.$tr$),
('40c88db7-dc95-4893-88a2-8d774d33fd36', 'en', $tr$The one who sings over you$tr$, $tr$The prophet describes the Lord rejoicing over his people, even singing because of them. We usually think of singing to God; here the image is reversed: someone sings for our sake. One possible reading is that there are people who rejoice over you even when you think you haven't done enough.$tr$, $tr$Can you picture yourself being a source of joy to someone, without needing to prove anything?$tr$, $tr$Listen to a song you like as a gift, without doing anything else at the same time.$tr$, $tr$Lord, it's strange to think that you rejoice over me. Help me receive this and rest a little from the pressure I put on myself.$tr$),
('436423d4-b655-450b-a839-38264ab0d221', 'en', $tr$The day of silence$tr$, $tr$After the crucifixion, the women prepare what they need and rest on the Sabbath, as the law required. It's a day with no event, of grief and waiting. One possible reading is that there are days when nothing seems to happen, and the text recognizes that silence as part of the story: not every day is a day of answers.$tr$, $tr$In what area of your life are you in a Saturday, waiting without knowing what comes next?$tr$, $tr$Set aside a few minutes of silence today, without trying to solve anything.$tr$, $tr$Lord, there are silences in my life that weigh heavily. Teach me to wait with you, even without answers.$tr$),
('44ce6ca2-18c8-4bfd-8b63-1e460f74bee1', 'en', $tr$Light for those walking in darkness$tr$, $tr$The prophet speaks of a people who lived in darkness and saw a great light. The promise doesn't begin with those who were already doing well: it begins with those who walked without seeing the way. On this first day of Advent, one possible reading is that waiting means lighting a small light and continuing to walk, even without seeing everything ahead.$tr$, $tr$Think of an area of your life that seems to be in the dark right now. What would be a small light for just today?$tr$, $tr$Light a candle or a low light for a few minutes and spend that time in silence.$tr$, $tr$Lord, there are parts of my life that seem to be in the dark. Thank you that the light reaches me too. Help me wait with an open heart.$tr$),
('452bfb7a-9b96-4e72-8480-d26ec872a63d', 'en', $tr$There is a time for everything$tr$, $tr$Ecclesiastes says there is a right time for every purpose under heaven. For someone who lives in a rush, that's a relief: not everything is for now, and not everything fits into one day. Recognizing the season you're in helps you stop demanding from yourself what doesn't belong to this season.$tr$, $tr$What season are you in right now: planting, waiting, resting, starting over?$tr$, $tr$Cross one thing off today's list that can wait for another time.$tr$, $tr$Father, teach me to recognize the season I'm in. Free me from the hurry to live everything at once.$tr$),
('473261d1-1b37-45e3-a522-f4d1b6f5ea0a', 'en', $tr$No room, but born$tr$, $tr$Jesus is born in the middle of a forced journey, with no room at the inn, in a manger. Christmas begins without any comfort at all. One possible reading is that the text contradicts the idea that something important happens only when conditions are perfect. He arrived improvised, and that was enough.$tr$, $tr$What part of your Christmas isn't how you planned it? Can you receive the improvised without judging yourself?$tr$, $tr$Accept something unplanned today with good humor.$tr$, $tr$Lord, nothing is quite as I wanted this Christmas. Help me receive what has come, the way it came, with gratitude.$tr$),
('475bccce-07da-4a8a-ad79-13edcf500391', 'en', $tr$Stopping is not wasting time$tr$, $tr$Psalm 46:10 gives a direct invitation: "Be still". It doesn't talk about producing more, praying more beautifully, or understanding faster — it talks about stopping. If your day was hectic and this moment is the only breather you've had, it has already done what it needed to do. It doesn't have to produce anything more than that.$tr$, $tr$Notice whether today you measured the value of your day by how much you produced. Two minutes of quiet between one task and the next isn't wasted time — it's the opposite of that.$tr$, $tr$Choose a moment today to stay two minutes doing nothing productive — no phone, no task, just still. No guilt afterward.$tr$, $tr$Help me stop for a moment without feeling that I'm wasting time. Sometimes what I need isn't one more thing to do; it's just to breathe.$tr$),
('49f12dc6-809e-4b46-a9ca-3ad7cfa72f58', 'en', $tr$Love in practice$tr$, $tr$Jesus gives a new command: to love one another as he has loved us. Love here is something you can see: it shows up in actions, not only in feelings. Loving well starts with small gestures, toward the people close to us, in everyday life.$tr$, $tr$Who in your home or at work needs a concrete gesture of care today?$tr$, $tr$Do something concrete for someone today: cook something, help out, or listen.$tr$, $tr$Father, I want to love through actions, not only with words. Show me who needs me today.$tr$),
('5050435c-5a31-4102-a154-649f730cfebe', 'en', $tr$Anxiety is not a lack of faith$tr$, $tr$Philippians 4:6-7 speaks of bringing your worries to God in prayer, "in every situation". It isn't a measure of how anxious you're allowed to be — it's an invitation to put the weight somewhere instead of carrying it alone. If your mind is full today, that doesn't disqualify you from anything. This is exactly what the passage was written for.$tr$, $tr$If your mind is full today, notice the difference between "I'm anxious" and "I'm a person of little faith". They are very different things, and only the first one is true.$tr$, $tr$Write in one sentence the worry that weighs most on you today. Just that — you don't need to solve it, only to name it.$tr$, $tr$I'm bringing to you what's weighing on my mind today. I don't have to solve everything alone right now — I just need to put this somewhere.$tr$),
('53f902ca-8a65-4fec-bd7b-b041ff1bd1ca', 'en', $tr$Do not be afraid$tr$, $tr$The angel tells Mary not to be afraid, because she has found favor with God. Before giving the plan, the angel calms her. In our lives too there are invitations that frighten us. One possible reading is that hearing "do not be afraid" doesn't erase the fright, but gives permission to move forward anyway.$tr$, $tr$What news, change, or invitation scares you right now? Would a small step help?$tr$, $tr$Take the first step on something you've been avoiding out of fear, even if it's small.$tr$, $tr$Father, I'm afraid of some things that lie ahead. Remind me that I'm not alone, and give me courage for the next step.$tr$),
('55251e8a-0335-4645-abf5-b6a78072c00f', 'en', $tr$The sky also speaks$tr$, $tr$The psalm says the heavens declare the glory of God and the sky proclaims the work of his hands, day after day, night after night. One possible reading is that creation is a language without words: not everything needs an explanation to be felt. Looking up can also be a form of prayer.$tr$, $tr$When was the last time you stopped to look at the sky?$tr$, $tr$Look at the sky for one minute today, without doing anything else.$tr$, $tr$Lord, thank you for a creation that speaks without words. Help me stop from time to time and notice.$tr$),
('5579d1e7-bb06-4ce0-ba5f-addc6a1e1233', 'en', $tr$Mary's joy$tr$, $tr$Mary, still not knowing what lay ahead, begins a song saying that her soul magnifies the Lord and her spirit rejoices in God. Her joy doesn't come from having everything worked out, but from recognizing that she was looked upon with tenderness. One possible reading is that joy can be born from feeling seen and cared for.$tr$, $tr$What this week shows you that you were cared for, even without having everything figured out?$tr$, $tr$Write down three things you're thankful for today, even the small ones.$tr$, $tr$Lord, thank you for looking on me with tenderness. May my joy come from knowing I'm cared for, not only from things going well.$tr$),
('55cef37e-0d40-4911-af44-bb76494a3963', 'en', $tr$Treasuring in the heart$tr$, $tr$After everything, the text says that Mary kept all these things and pondered them in her heart. At the end of a year full of events, this gesture is a model: not everything needs to be talked about or resolved. You can keep things, think slowly, and let the meaning appear over time.$tr$, $tr$What from this year do you want to keep tenderly in your heart?$tr$, $tr$Write down three moments from the year that deserve to be remembered.$tr$, $tr$Lord, thank you for this year. Help me keep what was good, learn from what was hard, and enter the next one with hope.$tr$),
('56765833-ece1-4ef5-837b-9d876472fab4', 'en', $tr$A different kind of peace$tr$, $tr$Jesus tells his disciples that he leaves them his peace, and that he doesn't give it as the world gives. The world's peace often depends on things going well. The peace in this text lives alongside problems: it asks that our hearts not be troubled or afraid. It isn't pretending to be calm; it's resting in the one who takes care of us.$tr$, $tr$Where does your peace depend too much on everything going right?$tr$, $tr$When you feel anxious today, take three deep breaths and say: I don't need to solve everything right now.$tr$, $tr$Father, the peace I look for often depends on circumstances. Give me a steadier peace, one that stays even when things don't go as I planned.$tr$),
('5b4c9396-04d4-4119-ba2c-a0ce35e38ad9', 'en', $tr$Courage is not the absence of fear$tr$, $tr$The command to be strong and courageous, given to Joshua at a time of great change, comes with a promise of companionship: the Lord would be with him wherever he went. The courage of this text doesn't come from not feeling fear, but from not facing it alone.$tr$, $tr$Is there something you've been putting off out of fear? Taking the first step doesn't require having lost the fear.$tr$, $tr$Do one small thing today that you've been avoiding.$tr$, $tr$Father, I'm afraid of some things that lie ahead. Remind me that I don't walk alone, and give me courage for the next step.$tr$),
('5cc8cba9-5004-4865-a9aa-5cb7b2404f4a', 'en', $tr$He is not here$tr$, $tr$The women go to the tomb and find the stone rolled away. They hear: why do you look for the living among the dead? He is not here; he has risen! The news comes by surprise, at daybreak. For those who have followed this week, today is a day of joy. One possible reading is that the last word of the story was not pain.$tr$, $tr$What in your life have you been treating as if there were no way forward anymore? What if there were still a morning ahead?$tr$, $tr$Celebrate today: hug someone, share a meal, call someone dear to you. Singing counts too.$tr$, $tr$Lord, thank you for the morning of the resurrection. May this hope reach the parts of my life that seemed closed.$tr$),
('5ea91133-8628-49d3-b1c8-3ce22e83e3e2', 'en', $tr$Waiting like someone waiting for the dawn$tr$, $tr$The psalmist compares his waiting to that of a watchman who waits through the night for the morning. The watchman knows the morning will come; he just doesn't know the exact hour. It's a tired waiting, but one with direction. Waiting for Christmas, in this sense, means living through the night without forgetting that the day is coming.$tr$, $tr$What have you been waiting for lately? Can you wait without wearing yourself out, simply by keeping your eyes open?$tr$, $tr$Write down one thing you're waiting for and one thing you can do while you wait.$tr$, $tr$Father, waiting sometimes wears me out. Teach me to wait without rushing and without giving up, like someone who knows the morning is coming.$tr$),
('7039cf6e-c03d-4bac-9fde-48cb1cbbbddf', 'en', $tr$A mind renewed little by little$tr$, $tr$Paul speaks of not being conformed to the pattern of this world, and of being transformed by the renewing of your mind. Renewing is a verb of process: it happens little by little, not all at once. Small choices repeated over time usually weigh more than big resolutions.$tr$, $tr$Is there a thought that keeps repeating in your head that you'd like to answer in a different way?$tr$, $tr$Notice a thought that comes back today and write one true and kind sentence in response to it.$tr$, $tr$Father, I want to think with more clarity and less weight. Keep renewing my mind little by little, and give me patience with the process.$tr$),
('716dd92b-13e1-4200-ac30-08ec17c2c8e6', 'en', $tr$Joy as strength$tr$, $tr$The people were weeping as they heard God's law, and the leader tells them not to grieve, because the joy of the Lord is their strength. It was a day of celebration and sharing, with food for those who had nothing. One possible reading is that joy gives the energy to begin again, and it grows best when it is shared.$tr$, $tr$Who could you share something good with this year-end?$tr$, $tr$Share a meal, a sweet, or some time with someone today.$tr$, $tr$Lord, let joy be strength for me, and let me share it with the people near me. Help me remember those who are left out of the celebrations.$tr$),
('7aa603db-93d3-4b78-a322-7baec4c6410e', 'en', $tr$Let peace decide$tr$, $tr$Paul asks that the peace of Christ act as an umpire in the heart, helping us decide what to do, and he connects this with being thankful. In a week of rush and end-of-year commitments, it's a useful image: when you don't know whether to accept or decline something, which option leaves your heart more at peace?$tr$, $tr$Which commitment of this season did you accept out of obligation, and is it taking away your peace?$tr$, $tr$Choose one commitment you can decline or simplify, and do it without guilt.$tr$, $tr$Lord, I want to decide with more calm this year-end. May your peace help me say yes and no at the right moments.$tr$),
('8aaddfe2-785a-45ac-ad31-33de514641fc', 'en', $tr$Palms and hosannas$tr$, $tr$Jesus enters Jerusalem riding a donkey, and the crowd spreads cloaks and branches along the road, shouting hosanna. It's a scene of celebration, in contrast with what would come in the following days. One possible reading is that the text invites us to begin the week with attention: joy and pain stand very close together in this story.$tr$, $tr$What do you expect from this week? Can you enter it with an open heart?$tr$, $tr$Set aside five minutes each day this week to read the story of Jesus calmly.$tr$, $tr$Lord, I want to follow this week closely. Open my heart to what it wants to show me.$tr$),
('8cf11d18-5527-4b8c-b40f-6156980d690b', 'en', $tr$Where your thoughts go$tr$, $tr$Paul suggests filling the mind with what is true, noble, right, pure, lovely, and admirable. It's a guideline about focus, not an order to ignore problems. Thoughts tend to go where we take them most often.$tr$, $tr$Where did your mind spend the most energy today?$tr$, $tr$Write down three good and true things from your day.$tr$, $tr$Father, help me direct my thoughts toward what is good and true, without denying what is hard.$tr$),
('8eb1e3be-7a5c-4c62-8dac-8f50d138546d', 'en', $tr$New every morning$tr$, $tr$This text comes from a book of lament and yet it speaks of mercies renewed every morning. It doesn't deny the pain: it recognizes that we can start the day without carrying the full weight of yesterday. One possible reading for Advent is that every morning is a small new beginning, offered to us.$tr$, $tr$What from yesterday can you leave behind today?$tr$, $tr$When you wake up tomorrow, before you pick up your phone, say quietly: today is a new day.$tr$, $tr$Father, thank you for new mornings. Help me let go of what has already passed and start the day calmly.$tr$),
('90e5d552-4ec4-4ea9-8d8d-aea4339f36a7', 'en', $tr$Gratitude in small doses$tr$, $tr$Paul speaks of rejoicing, praying without ceasing, and giving thanks in all circumstances. Notice the preposition: in all circumstances, not for all of them. One possible reading is that the text doesn't ask us to pretend everything is fine, but to look for something to be thankful for even in the middle of what isn't.$tr$, $tr$What good thing, even a small one, happened today?$tr$, $tr$Give thanks out loud for three simple things from your day.$tr$, $tr$Father, even on hard days I want to recognize what is good. Open my eyes to the small things I can be thankful for.$tr$),
('93aa3110-306d-40db-b8e3-ce45f95f6dd9', 'en', $tr$Love that gives$tr$, $tr$This is one of the best-known verses in the Bible: God loved the world so much that he gave his one and only Son. The important verb is give. Love here isn't only a feeling; it's a gift. Christmas, in this reading, is the day love took the form of someone who came, instead of only advising from afar.$tr$, $tr$What act of love, not only words, can you do for someone this week?$tr$, $tr$Give a gift that costs no money: your time, your listening, or your help with a task.$tr$, $tr$Lord, thank you for a love that became present. Help me love through actions, not only with words.$tr$),
('986cc61e-a693-49b6-955e-23dd1acfe833', 'en', $tr$Remember at the table$tr$, $tr$At the last supper, Jesus breaks the bread and passes the cup, asking the disciples to do this in remembrance of him. It's a simple gesture, with food and a table. One possible reading is that remembering isn't only thinking: it's doing something together. Sharing a meal with attention is already a way of keeping what matters.$tr$, $tr$When was the last time you shared a meal with someone, without hurry and without a screen?$tr$, $tr$Have a meal today without your phone and with attention to the people at the table. If you're alone, eat with gratitude.$tr$, $tr$Father, thank you for the tables where I found company. Help me value the people who sit with me.$tr$),
('99661f27-be31-4890-bc3f-d2ad4baa976d', 'en', $tr$God with us$tr$, $tr$Matthew explains that Jesus' birth fulfills the promise of a son called Immanuel, which means God with us. In this reading, Christmas isn't just a date: it's the affirmation that God chose to be near, in the middle of human messiness, and not only up above. Anyone who feels alone can lean on this promise.$tr$, $tr$In what situation do you feel most the lack of company? Can you remember that you are not alone?$tr$, $tr$Tell someone: I'm here with you. And if you're the one who needs it, ask for company.$tr$, $tr$Lord, thank you for not staying far away. Help me notice your presence in ordinary days and in difficult ones.$tr$),
('9a660871-c198-411e-bc83-9a314a57eba2', 'en', $tr$Light without a spotlight$tr$, $tr$Jesus tells his listeners that they are the light of the world, and that no one lights a lamp to hide it, but to light up the house. The image is simple and useful, without spectacle: light serves those who are nearby. Good deeds done simply already fulfill that role.$tr$, $tr$Who close to you could benefit from a small help today?$tr$, $tr$Do something useful for someone without telling anyone.$tr$, $tr$Father, I want to be a simple and useful light to the people near me. Help me serve without needing applause.$tr$),
('9c9606f2-039f-4c87-8d42-99c98af645f1', 'en', $tr$Peace and justice hand in hand$tr$, $tr$The psalm imagines love and faithfulness meeting, and justice and peace kissing. The peace the text describes doesn't ignore what is wrong: it walks together with justice. In Advent, this can remind us that building peace includes caring for those at a disadvantage, not only avoiding conflict.$tr$, $tr$Is there someone nearby being treated unfairly by people you have some influence over?$tr$, $tr$Give something or spend time with someone who is in need this week.$tr$, $tr$Father, let my peace not be indifference. Help me notice who needs justice and care, and act on what is within my reach.$tr$),
('a2e5bf58-e0df-4502-b53b-acb26d2f54cd', 'en', $tr$A child is given to us$tr$, $tr$The text announces that a boy would be born, and that his name would include titles such as Counselor and Prince of Peace. It's striking: the hope of peace comes in the form of a child, not an army. One possible reading is that the peace the text speaks of begins small and fragile, and grows with care.$tr$, $tr$What small peace can you look after today, at home or at work?$tr$, $tr$Do one thing that eases tension at home or at work: an apology, a thank-you, or a gentle silence.$tr$, $tr$Lord, I want to be someone who looks after peace around me. Help me choose words and attitudes that calm things down.$tr$),
('a83591b2-96ec-4af3-a0cf-d14344cd562f', 'en', $tr$Where help comes from$tr$, $tr$The psalmist lifts his eyes to the hills and asks where his help will come from. Asking is honest: the text doesn't pretend it doesn't need help. The answer comes right after, pointing to the Lord, who made heaven and earth. Recognizing that we need help is also part of the journey.$tr$, $tr$Where are you trying to handle everything on your own?$tr$, $tr$Ask someone for help with something small today, or accept help that has already been offered.$tr$, $tr$Father, I lift my eyes today looking for help. Teach me to ask when I need to, and to trust that I'm not alone.$tr$),
('a97d68e4-d100-4494-8c46-7eeffa743e68', 'en', $tr$Counting the days$tr$, $tr$The psalmist asks to be taught to number his days, so that his heart may gain wisdom. Days are limited, and counting them is a way of paying attention, not of living in anxiety. When we notice time, we tend to choose better what to do with it.$tr$, $tr$What important thing have you been leaving for when you have spare time?$tr$, $tr$Decide what is worth doing with the next 30 free minutes of your day, and protect that time.$tr$, $tr$Lord, teach me to value my days, without hurry and without waste. Help me choose well.$tr$),
('b264bd2b-5443-442e-a50c-7188e33c9323', 'en', $tr$Nothing can separate us$tr$, $tr$Paul says he is convinced that nothing — neither death nor life, neither the present nor the future, nor any created thing — can separate us from the love of God in Christ Jesus. For those who have days when they feel distant, the text speaks of a relationship that doesn't depend on performance.$tr$, $tr$When did you feel far away, and how was it to come back?$tr$, $tr$Write a sentence for yourself to remember on the days when you feel distant.$tr$, $tr$Lord, on the days I feel far away, remind me that your love doesn't depend on my performance.$tr$),
('b3bd3f22-b9cd-4889-aa7f-626e7d1dd61d', 'en', $tr$Gifts and worship$tr$, $tr$The magi travel to find the child, rejoice when they see the star, and give gifts. The text highlights their joy and their worship, before the gifts. One possible reading is that giving gifts is a way of saying thank you, and that presence and attention matter more than the price.$tr$, $tr$What gift could you give that says: I thought of you?$tr$, $tr$Write a short note to someone saying what you value in that person.$tr$, $tr$Lord, may my gifts be a sign of care, not of comparison. Help me give with joy and without pressure.$tr$),
('b3e732cb-45fc-423a-a851-c731231954d7', 'en', $tr$The Word became flesh$tr$, $tr$John sums up Christmas in a different way: the Word became flesh and dwelt among us, full of grace and truth. Instead of a scene with shepherds, there's an idea: what God wanted to say to the world came in the form of a person, with a body and an everyday life. One possible reading is that God wanted to be understood up close.$tr$, $tr$When did you realize that someone was the best explanation of what love is for you?$tr$, $tr$Thank someone who has shown you what love is through their actions.$tr$, $tr$Father, thank you for coming close. Help me also be a concrete form of love for the people near me.$tr$),
('b7262886-b208-48d2-ab4b-022b3373cc0b', 'en', $tr$Praying without hiding the fear$tr$, $tr$In the garden, Jesus steps away and prays honestly: if it is possible, let this cup pass, but let your will be done. He doesn't pretend to be fine. One possible reading is that praying honestly, including the fear and the wish to escape, is part of faith. If you're going through a very heavy moment, talk with someone you trust as well.$tr$, $tr$What have you been avoiding saying in prayer because you think it isn't appropriate?$tr$, $tr$Today, say in prayer, in your own words, what weighs on you most, without dressing it up.$tr$, $tr$Lord, I don't want to pretend before you. I say honestly what I feel, and I trust that you hear me.$tr$),
('b7cbe2c1-3e04-4b59-a49f-85b68da1d812', 'en', $tr$The small also holds a promise$tr$, $tr$The prophet points to Bethlehem, a small town, as the place from which someone important would come. The promise doesn't choose the most famous address. This can remind us that places and moments that seem small, like a simple home or an ordinary day, can also hold something great.$tr$, $tr$Which small part of your routine do you tend to overlook? What if it matters more than it seems?$tr$, $tr$Do one small, ordinary task today with full attention and without hurry.$tr$, $tr$Father, help me see value in the small things of my day. May I not despise the simple.$tr$),
('b8da486b-ffc2-46d5-921d-77f8979712b4', 'en', $tr$Love came first$tr$, $tr$The text states that love didn't start with us: God loved first and sent his Son. We don't need to earn that love before receiving it. That takes away a weight: it isn't a test you have to pass. You can live Christmas as someone who receives something before having done anything to deserve it.$tr$, $tr$Where in your life do you feel you have to earn love? What would change if you had already received it?$tr$, $tr$Today, accept a compliment or a gesture of care without justifying yourself. Just say thank you.$tr$, $tr$Father, it's hard to believe that you loved me first. Help me rest in that love and stop trying to earn it.$tr$),
('bd766394-3905-484d-b774-0483c1f30d5c', 'en', $tr$An invitation for the weary$tr$, $tr$Jesus invites the weary and burdened to come to him, and speaks of rest, of a light yoke, and of learning from one who is gentle and humble in heart. It is an invitation, not a demand. If you came here tired, you're already in the place of those who were called.$tr$, $tr$What is weighing on you most this week? Naming the weight is already a first step.$tr$, $tr$Write in one sentence what is wearing you out, and read it quietly, as if handing it over.$tr$, $tr$Lord, I come tired and without much to offer. Thank you for receiving me like this. Teach me to rest in you.$tr$),
('bed00af9-0a46-4072-a044-3139f22f5693', 'en', $tr$Praise and peace$tr$, $tr$Suddenly a multitude of angels appears, praising God and speaking of peace among people. After the announcement comes the celebration. The praise in the text is a response to something that has already happened. Today you can celebrate in a simple way: sing, give thanks, or stay in quiet wonder. Each person has their own way.$tr$, $tr$How do you like to give thanks: singing, praying, writing, or in silence?$tr$, $tr$Give thanks in your own way for a few minutes, without hurry.$tr$, $tr$Lord, may my thanksgiving be sincere, in my own way. Thank you for the peace you announced.$tr$),
('c10df744-8f85-4132-8611-ff8cf38abe17', 'en', $tr$The joy that leaps$tr$, $tr$When Mary arrives at Elizabeth's house, Elizabeth's baby moves with joy, and she says that Mary is blessed. It's a meeting between two women carrying unlikely promises. Joy here happens during a visit: someone shows up and the day changes. Visiting is also a way of bringing Christmas.$tr$, $tr$Who haven't you visited in a long time? A short visit can change the day for both of you.$tr$, $tr$Visit or call someone who has been alone.$tr$, $tr$Father, may I bring joy to every place I go. Help me make a visit or a phone call into a gift.$tr$),
('c173537d-e355-494b-9636-78de3599cff9', 'en', $tr$The basics are enough$tr$, $tr$Micah sums up what the Lord asks: to act justly, to love mercy, and to walk humbly with God. A short list, without complicated rituals. For someone who always feels they owe something, it's a reminder that what matters most fits in a sentence.$tr$, $tr$Of these three things, which is the hardest to practice in this season?$tr$, $tr$Choose one of the three and take a concrete action on it today.$tr$, $tr$Father, help me live what is essential: to act justly, to love mercy, and to walk humbly with you.$tr$),
('c67ae2dd-9d07-4068-b8e0-93c82237fa9d', 'en', $tr$Listen first$tr$, $tr$James advises us to be quick to listen, slow to speak, and slow to become angry. These are three short pauses that can change the direction of a conversation. Really listening is a form of care: it tells the other person they matter even before they've been answered.$tr$, $tr$In which conversations do you tend to answer before the other person has finished?$tr$, $tr$In a conversation today, listen until the end before you respond.$tr$, $tr$Father, teach me to listen carefully and speak with care. May I not be in a hurry to answer or to defend myself.$tr$),
('c8439d2b-799b-4ef0-a002-056ddc12b46b', 'en', $tr$Strength and peace$tr$, $tr$The psalm ends with a short line: the Lord gives strength to his people and blesses them with peace. Strength and peace appear together, as if one holds up the other. Those who have peace bear the day better; those who have strength can look after peace. One possible reading is that both are received as gifts, not won by force.$tr$, $tr$What do you need more today: strength or peace? You can ask for both.$tr$, $tr$Take a five-minute break today, with no screen and no task.$tr$, $tr$Father, I need strength for what is coming and peace for what has already passed. Give me both, in the amount I can bear today.$tr$),
('cb3d638f-5f3b-40ea-94e3-41b7a6ddd47e', 'en', $tr$Presence before tasks$tr$, $tr$Martha welcomes Jesus into her home and busies herself with many tasks; Mary sits down to listen to him. Jesus tells Martha that she is worried and distracted by many things, and that Mary has chosen the better part. It isn't a text against serving, nor about who is better: it's about not letting tasks take the place of presence.$tr$, $tr$In what moments do the day's tasks take the place of people, or of rest?$tr$, $tr$Share a meal or a conversation today without looking at your phone.$tr$, $tr$Lord, I have many things to do and I get lost in them. Help me be fully present for what matters.$tr$),
('cdd2a331-4b5f-4af8-96da-3f4eadf67ee8', 'en', $tr$The grain that falls$tr$, $tr$Jesus compares his own mission to a grain of wheat that falls to the ground and dies in order to bear much fruit. The image is uncomfortable: it speaks of losing in order to gain. One possible reading is that some good things are born only after something has ended. That doesn't make loss easy, but it gives meaning to some goodbyes.$tr$, $tr$What ending in your life might be making room for something new?$tr$, $tr$Let go today of something that has already served its time: a habit, an object, a small hurt.$tr$, $tr$Father, help me accept the endings that come and wait for the fruit they may bring. Give me patience with the process.$tr$),
('d12d6d44-12a7-450c-8e0b-d21081d382a7', 'en', $tr$Peace that guards the heart$tr$, $tr$Paul suggests exchanging anxiety for prayer and thanksgiving, and speaks of a peace that guards the heart and mind. The image is that of a sentry protecting. It isn't a promise that problems disappear, but that the heart doesn't have to stay alone in the middle of them. If anxiety weighs heavily, it's worth talking with someone you trust or with a professional.$tr$, $tr$Which worry can you bring to prayer today, instead of replaying it in your head?$tr$, $tr$Write one worry on a piece of paper, pray over it, and put the paper away so you stop going over it again and again.$tr$, $tr$Lord, I hand over to you the worries I can't solve. Guard my heart and my thoughts with your peace.$tr$),
('dcd3d894-78ca-422e-88bb-ff4d7420b510', 'en', $tr$Joy that does not depend on the day$tr$, $tr$Paul writes from prison and still says: rejoice in the Lord always. It isn't an order to feel joy all the time; coming from someone who was imprisoned, it sounds more like an invitation to remember what joy rests on. On hard days, joy can be small: a hot coffee, a message, a short prayer.$tr$, $tr$If today was a hard day, what is one small reason to be thankful?$tr$, $tr$Do something simple you enjoy, just for pleasure, and notice how it feels.$tr$, $tr$Father, not every day is easy. Help me find small reasons for joy and remember where it comes from.$tr$),
('e4316007-0425-4aae-9a81-4aa2bcb81fcf', 'en', $tr$Clothe yourself in kindness$tr$, $tr$Paul speaks of clothing yourself with compassion, kindness, humility, gentleness, and patience, bearing with one another and forgiving, and above all, love. We choose our clothes in the morning: you can decide which attitude to wear before you leave home, even on days when it doesn't come on its own.$tr$, $tr$Who will you be with today who calls for a little more patience?$tr$, $tr$Choose one attitude from the list and practice it in a conversation today.$tr$, $tr$Lord, help me to clothe myself with compassion and patience today, especially with the people who wear me out. May love be the first piece.$tr$),
('e697c76e-4606-47d3-a36c-44002e2079fa', 'en', $tr$The morning visit$tr$, $tr$Zechariah speaks of a light from on high that visits those who live in darkness, to guide their steps into the way of peace. It is Christmas Eve: the light of the first day of Advent reaches the end of the waiting. Today you can look back and notice that the way has been lit, step by step.$tr$, $tr$Looking back over these weeks, what do you notice has been illuminated or has changed in you?$tr$, $tr$Before you sleep, give thanks for one thing from each week of Advent: hope, peace, joy, and love.$tr$, $tr$Father, thank you for this walk toward Christmas. May your light guide my steps along the way of peace.$tr$),
('f03f5c3f-32ff-4f9c-82a5-5c431d2541c1', 'en', $tr$Faith that walks in the dark$tr$, $tr$Hebrews describes faith as the confidence in what we hope for and the assurance about what we do not see. Those who trust don't always see the outcome before they walk. One possible reading is that doubt and faith can live side by side: the text speaks of moving forward, not of never hesitating.$tr$, $tr$What are you hoping for that you don't see yet?$tr$, $tr$Write down one thing you're hoping for and offer it in prayer, in your own words.$tr$, $tr$Lord, help my faith when it wavers. Give me courage to keep going even without seeing everything.$tr$),
('f6b18efb-8c47-4034-b98d-cc748c9fa4cf', 'en', $tr$Joy after the waiting$tr$, $tr$The psalm says that those who sow in tears will reap with songs. The image is of a farmer who plants in sorrow and, after a while, returns with his arms full. The text doesn't rush the process: between the tears and the harvest there is a time of waiting. For someone in the middle of that road, this can be a relief.$tr$, $tr$What have you planted with effort that you haven't yet seen grow?$tr$, $tr$Take one small step today on something you've been working on: a study, a relationship, a habit.$tr$, $tr$Father, some of the things I plant haven't borne fruit yet. Give me patience to wait, and eyes to see what is already growing.$tr$),
('faac4684-5264-49d1-b818-05a8b673b2d9', 'en', $tr$Hope that overflows$tr$, $tr$Paul wishes that hope would bring joy and peace to those who trust. The important word is overflow: what fills beyond measure ends up reaching those nearby. Hope here isn't kept to oneself; it passes from person to person through small gestures, like a kind word or a visit.$tr$, $tr$Who around you could receive a little hope this week?$tr$, $tr$Send a message of care to someone who has been feeling discouraged.$tr$, $tr$Lord, fill my heart with hope, and let it reach the people near me too. May I be a source of encouragement to someone today.$tr$)
insert into public.reading_plan_translations (plan_id, language, title, description) values
('38da0609-f0a4-4db4-ad02-ad808456191f', 'en', $tr$30 days with Lumi$tr$, $tr$A full month, one devotional a day: rest, courage, relationships, and gratitude in one journey.$tr$),
('38a8d833-bfd0-4c4c-ae83-4a8d6f23bc5b', 'en', $tr$Advent with Lumi$tr$, $tr$24 days of waiting and hope until Christmas: a week of hope, a week of peace, a week of joy, and a week of love.$tr$),
('634c1691-bf4b-4cb0-95da-0991b2196605', 'en', $tr$Courage and a fresh start$tr$, $tr$Seven days for anyone who needs to begin again, find strength in weakness, and keep moving forward.$tr$),
('81ef0320-8f32-46ac-92b1-571bd2e8ee81', 'en', $tr$Rest for the weary$tr$, $tr$Seven days to stop without guilt: rest, wait, and let the day be the size it is.$tr$),
('b725f668-98ce-49f2-9f3f-15716d48f055', 'en', $tr$Gratitude and purpose$tr$, $tr$Six days to give thanks, notice what is good, and carry each day's work as an offering.$tr$),
('b07c3deb-32ac-431c-8b29-00da0397be7f', 'en', $tr$Less anxiety, more trust$tr$, $tr$Seven days to hand over your worries, look after your thoughts, and trust without having all the answers.$tr$),
('093e0a22-ac78-47a2-bac5-8c4a883876b6', 'en', $tr$Christmas: seven days$tr$, $tr$The manger, the shepherds, the angels, the magi, and what Mary treasured in her heart, over seven days.$tr$),
('f0f9b126-c131-4588-afb0-f6c14a231a97', 'en', $tr$Listen, forgive, love$tr$, $tr$Seven days on how we treat people: listening first, answering with calm, forgiving, and loving in practice.$tr$),
('761e8374-a214-4bac-b915-bbb63e3448d3', 'en', $tr$Easter week$tr$, $tr$Eight days, from Palm Sunday to Easter Sunday: serving, praying, silence, and the morning of the resurrection.$tr$)
insert into public.challenge_translations (challenge_id, language, title, description) values
('dcc6ac90-3d60-42f3-9407-c1760cf638fc', 'en', $tr$Five out of seven$tr$, $tr$Do the moment on 5 of the next 7 days. You get 2 rest days, with no pressure.$tr$),
('47814a42-6d64-4ec5-9bca-ee979b299280', 'en', $tr$Together for a week$tr$, $tr$The circle adds up to 4 moments per person in 7 days. It's a goal for everyone together, with no ranking.$tr$),
('4be72616-4c68-4e31-b883-36579088c00b', 'en', $tr$Praying together$tr$, $tr$Each person prays for 2 requests in 7 days. The goal belongs to the circle, not to any one person alone.$tr$),
('0db29e48-a0fc-4884-a640-b075a4202375', 'en', $tr$Praying for others$tr$, $tr$Pray for 3 prayer requests from your circle in 7 days.$tr$),
('c8fe3985-852b-4eb6-a2ce-5cb3b63326e9', 'en', $tr$Week of consistency$tr$, $tr$Do the moment on 7 different days this week. If you miss one, Lumi understands and you can try again.$tr$),
('e4ee33bd-1b6e-42d8-af6e-bb056c92ab26', 'en', $tr$Week of reflection$tr$, $tr$Write 3 reflections at the end of your moments over 7 days. Only you see what you write.$tr$),
('d54a54fb-34dd-4068-9ad1-d7cd8442dc52', 'en', $tr$Week of cheering$tr$, $tr$Cheer for people in your circle 3 times in 7 days. A 💛 makes a difference.$tr$),
('53cbecf9-b0b8-4fe6-8736-723145f31c65', 'en', $tr$Cheering for everyone$tr$, $tr$Each person cheers 2 times in 7 days. Added together, the whole circle gets encouraged.$tr$),
('e712d9b6-e026-498e-984c-778b92454421', 'en', $tr$One month, twenty moments$tr$, $tr$Do the moment on 20 days within 30 days. A month's walk, at your own pace.$tr$)
